---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SOCIALS } from '../consts';

const skills = [
  'Enterprise & business architecture',
  'Agentic AI architecture & LLMOps',
  'AI governance & agent security',
  'Salesforce, CPQ & commerce',
  'Quote-to-cash & revenue recognition',
  'Sales planning & forecasting',
  'MDM, CDP & Snowflake',
  'Integration, APIs & event-driven design',
  'Architecture review & standards',
];
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Saurabh Rawane is an enterprise architect who designs the systems companies use to sell, bill and recognize revenue, and the AI agents that now work inside them."
>
  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Hi, I'm Saurabh.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I design the systems companies use to sell, bill and recognize revenue, and lately the AI
        agents that work inside them.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm an enterprise architect in the San Francisco Bay Area with a little over 20 years in
        go-to-market and finance systems: CRM, CPQ, commerce, marketing, customer success,
        quote-to-cash, revenue recognition and sales planning. For the last 15 of those years I've
        been at Autodesk, where I'm Chief Architect for GTM Platforms.
      </p>
      <p>
        My work sits where business models meet systems. When a company changes how it sells, the
        CRM, the quoting engine, billing and the revenue ledger all have to change with it, and
        still agree with each other afterwards. That is the problem I keep coming back to, and it is
        what most of this blog is about.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm working on now</h2>
      <p>
        Bringing AI agents into the revenue stack in a way an enterprise can trust. I lead the
        agentic AI work across Autodesk's GTM systems: agents for pipeline intelligence, account
        planning, forecasting and operations. The hard part is rarely the model. It is the platform
        around it: secure data access, evaluation, and a path from pilot to production that
        governance can sign off on.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also lead the enterprise strategy for integrating acquisitions across our marketing and
        CRM stacks: deciding what to consolidate and what to keep, and bringing customer, pipeline
        and revenue data together without disrupting the business that was just acquired.
      </p>

      <h2>Highlights</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Three business model transitions.</strong> Architected quote-to-cash and revenue
          recognition through Autodesk's moves from perpetual licenses to subscription, then Flex
          consumption, then a direct transaction model, keeping bookings, billing and revenue
          reconciled across Sales and Finance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI agents in production.</strong> Built a governed agent platform with tool
          orchestration, secure data access, evaluation and LLMOps, and shipped a touchless
          order-conversion agent on Salesforce Agentforce.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Architecture at scale.</strong> Chair the Architecture Review Board for 15+
          engineering teams across 8 domains, and lead a community of 20 solution leads, for systems
          serving 3,000+ sellers, 20,000+ partners and $7B in annual revenue.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Planning from one source of truth.</strong> Architected quota, account planning
          and forecasting on a single governed data foundation, shortening the planning cycle by a
          full quarter.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI before it was called that.</strong> Machine-learning case routing in Salesforce
          Omni-Channel and a Watson-powered support chatbot, years before LLMs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where it started.</strong> Force.com architect with Salesforce Professional
          Services, and technical lead for the Dreamforce portal from 2009 to 2011. Spoke at
          Dreamforce in 2010 and 2014.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What I write about</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>AI agents in go-to-market:</strong> <a href="/blog/before-the-handoff-ai-lead-development/">Before the Handoff</a> and <a href="/blog/below-the-line-ai-sdr-agent/">Below the Line</a></li>
        <li><strong>Revenue and forecasting data models:</strong> <a href="/blog/forecasting-renewals-expansion-downsell/">One Deal, Three Stories</a> and <a href="/blog/build-the-forecast-call-skill/">Build the Forecast Call</a></li>
        <li><strong>Architecture decisions:</strong> <a href="/blog/build-vs-buy-five-questions/">Build vs Buy</a></li>
        <li><strong>The Salesforce platform:</strong> <a href="/blog/aiforce-is-our-force-com-moment/">AIforce Is Our Force.com Moment</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section class="about-skills">
      <h2>Skills</h2>
      <ul class="chips">
        {skills.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="prose about-contact">
      <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        I'm always glad to compare notes on GTM architecture, revenue systems, or getting AI agents
        into production.
        The best way to reach me is {SOCIALS.linkedin && <a href={SOCIALS.linkedin} rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a>}{SOCIALS.email && <> or <a href={SOCIALS.email}>email</a></>}.
      </p>
      <p class="muted"><small>Views on this site are my own and don't represent my employer.</small></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about-skills {
    margin-top: 2.2em;
  }
  .about-skills h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0.25em;
  }
  .about-contact {
    margin-top: 2.2em;
  }
</style>
